//? Title: Alpha Max Plus Beta Min: A Square-Root-Free Magnitude
//? Subtitle: Closed-form minimax coefficients, the polygon generalisation, and edge strengths of handwritten digits with datamunge's algorithms::alpha_max_beta_min
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
The length of a vector, \(\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\), needs a square root. The
*alpha max plus beta min* estimate \(\alpha\max(|x|,|y|)+\beta\min(|x|,|y|)\)
replaces it with two multiplications and an addition. In polar form the
estimate is a cosine, and that turns the coefficient choice into
trigonometry. The pair with the smallest worst-case error is
\(\alpha=2\cos(\pi/8)/(1+\cos(\pi/8))\), \(\beta=2\sin(\pi/8)/(1+\cos(\pi/8))\),
and its error is exactly \(\tan^2(\pi/16)=3.96\%\). Allowing \(k\) linear
pieces instead of one gives error \(\tan^2(\pi/16k)\), which falls as
\(1/k^2\). We apply the estimate to the 100 139 non-zero Sobel gradient
vectors of the 1 797 images in `dm::datasets::digits()`. The worst error
observed matches the theory to four decimals. The mean error is \(+0.90\%\)
because gradient directions are spread over the whole octant. Applying a
threshold to the estimate instead of the true magnitude changes between
0.5% and 2% of edge decisions, depending on the threshold.
)

\toc

> The data: gradient vectors of handwritten digits

The digits data holds 1 797 handwritten digits as \(8\times8\) images with
intensities from 0 to 16. Edge detection starts from the image gradient. At
each pixel, `cv::sobel_gradient` estimates the horizontal and vertical
derivatives \((g_x,g_y)\), and the *edge strength* is the length
\(\sqrt{g_x^2+g_y^2}\). This is where alpha max plus beta min was born:
hardware that had to compute a magnitude per pixel or per radar sample,
with no fast square root.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::vector<double> gx, gy, len;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < digits.nrows(); ++r) {
    std::vector<double> img(64);
    for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i) img[i] = digits.double_at(std::format("pixel_{}", i), r);
    const auto g = dm::cv::sobel_gradient(img, 8, 8);
    for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i)
      if (g.gx[i] != 0 || g.gy[i] != 0) {
        gx.push_back(g.gx[i]);
        gy.push_back(g.gy[i]);
        len.push_back(std::hypot(g.gx[i], g.gy[i]));
      }
  }
  std::size_t axis = 0, diagonal = 0;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < gx.size(); ++i) {
    axis += (gx[i] == 0 || gy[i] == 0);
    diagonal += std::fabs(gx[i]) == std::fabs(gy[i]);
  }
  auto sorted = len;
  std::ranges::sort(sorted);
  std::print("| quantity | value |\n| :--- | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| images | {} |\n| pixels | {} |\n| non-zero gradient vectors | {} |\n", digits.nrows(),
             64 * digits.nrows(), gx.size());
  std::print("| along an axis (min component 0) | {} |\n| exactly diagonal (equal components) | {} |\n", axis,
             diagonal);
  std::print("| median edge strength | {:.3f} |\n| 90th percentile | {:.3f} |\n| maximum | {:.3f} |\n",
             sorted[sorted.size() / 2], sorted[sorted.size() * 9 / 10], sorted.back());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| quantity | value |
| :--- | ---: |
| images | 1797 |
| pixels | 115008 |
| non-zero gradient vectors | 100139 |
| along an axis (min component 0) | 3201 |
| exactly diagonal (equal components) | 10163 |
| median edge strength | 4.276 |
| 90th percentile | 7.302 |
| maximum | 8.839 |
// result_end

The 100 139 vectors are the test set for everything below. The Sobel
kernels on integer images give derivatives that are multiples of 1/8, so
exact ties are common, including over 10 000 vectors at exactly
\(45^\circ\).

> The method

>> The estimate in polar form

Write \(\text{hi}=\max(|x|,|y|)\) and \(\text{lo}=\min(|x|,|y|)\). By
symmetry every vector folds into the first octant: \(\text{hi}=r\cos\theta\)
and \(\text{lo}=r\sin\theta\) with \(r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\) and
\(0\le\theta\le\pi/4\). The ratio of estimate to truth is then

$$
\frac{\alpha\,\text{hi}+\beta\,\text{lo}}{r}=\alpha\cos\theta+\beta\sin\theta
=A\cos(\theta-\varphi),\qquad A=\sqrt{\alpha^2+\beta^2},\ \ \tan\varphi=\beta/\alpha .
$$
\alttext(The estimate divided by r equals alpha cos theta plus beta sin theta, which equals A cos of theta minus phi, where A is the square root of alpha squared plus beta squared and tan phi is beta over alpha.)

So the relative error depends only on the direction, and it traces a single
arch of a cosine over the octant. The arch peaks at \(\theta=\varphi\) with
height \(A\). Its lowest points are at the ends of the octant,
\(\theta=0\) (an axis) and \(\theta=\pi/4\) (a diagonal).

>> The minimax pair

To minimise the worst relative error \(\varepsilon\), the arch must be
centred, so that both ends sit equally low. Then \(\varphi=\pi/8\). The
ends sit at \(A\cos(\pi/8)\) and the peak at \(A\). Making the errors equal
and opposite,

$$
A\cos\tfrac{\pi}{8}=1-\varepsilon,\quad A=1+\varepsilon
\;\Longrightarrow\;
A=\frac{2}{1+\cos(\pi/8)},\qquad
\varepsilon=\frac{1-\cos(\pi/8)}{1+\cos(\pi/8)}=\tan^2\frac{\pi}{16}.
$$
\alttext(A cos pi over 8 equals 1 minus epsilon and A equals 1 plus epsilon, so A equals 2 over 1 plus cos pi over 8, and epsilon equals 1 minus cos pi over 8 over 1 plus cos pi over 8, which is tan squared of pi over 16.)

The last step is the half-angle identity
\((1-\cos u)/(1+\cos u)=\tan^2(u/2)\). Hence

$$
\alpha=\frac{2\cos(\pi/8)}{1+\cos(\pi/8)}=0.96043,\qquad
\beta=\frac{2\sin(\pi/8)}{1+\cos(\pi/8)}=0.39782,\qquad
\varepsilon=\tan^2\frac{\pi}{16}=0.039566 .
$$
\alttext(alpha equals 2 cos pi over 8 over 1 plus cos pi over 8, about 0.96043; beta equals 2 sin pi over 8 over 1 plus cos pi over 8, about 0.39782; epsilon equals tan squared pi over 16, about 0.039566.)

No other pair does better: any arch must be at least as wide as its height
variation over an octant, and centring minimises that height variation.

Other objectives give other pairs, all analysed by the same formula:

- *Never underestimate*: put the lowest points at exactly 1, so
  \(A\cos(\pi/8)=1\). This gives \(\alpha=1\), \(\beta=\tan(\pi/8)=0.41421\)
  and a worst error of \(1/\cos(\pi/8)-1=8.24\%\).
- *Least squares in angle*: minimise
  \(\int_0^{\pi/4}(\alpha\cos\theta+\beta\sin\theta-1)^2d\theta\). The
  integrals of \(\cos^2\), \(\sin\cos\), \(\sin^2\), \(\cos\) and \(\sin\)
  give \(2\times2\) normal equations.
- *Hardware-friendly* pairs such as \((1,\tfrac12)\), \((1,\tfrac14)\) and
  \((\tfrac{15}{16},\tfrac{15}{32})\), which need only shifts and adds.

`alpha_max_beta_min_error` evaluates any pair exactly, at the two ends and
at \(\varphi\) if it lies inside the octant.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/alpha_max_beta_min_ratio.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::tuple<std::string, alg::AlphaBetaCoefficients, dm::RGB>> pairs = {
      {"minimax", alg::alpha_max_beta_min_minimax(), dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}},
      {"least squares", alg::alpha_max_beta_min_least_squares(), dm::RGB{5, 150, 105}},
      {"never under", alg::alpha_max_beta_min_no_underestimate(), dm::RGB{245, 158, 11}},
      {"(1, 1/2)", {1.0, 0.5}, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}},
      {"(1, 1/4)", {1.0, 0.25}, dm::RGB{124, 58, 237}},
      {"(15/16, 15/32)", {15.0 / 16, 15.0 / 32}, dm::RGB{107, 114, 128}},
  };
  std::print("| pair | alpha | beta | lowest ratio | highest ratio | worst error |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  for (const auto& [name, c, colour] : pairs) {
    const auto e = alg::alpha_max_beta_min_error(c);
    std::print("| {} | {:.5f} | {:.5f} | {:.5f} | {:.5f} | {:.2f}% |\n", name, c.alpha, c.beta, e.min_ratio,
               e.max_ratio, 100 * e.peak_error);
    std::vector<double> deg, pct;
    for (int i = 0; i <= 90; ++i) {
      const double t = i * std::numbers::pi / 360;
      deg.push_back(i / 2.0);
      pct.push_back(100 * (alg::alpha_max_beta_min(std::cos(t), std::sin(t), c) - 1));
    }
    plot.lines(deg, pct, name, colour, name == "minimax" ? 3.0 : 1.5);
  }
  std::print("\ntan^2(pi/16) = {:.6f}.\n", std::pow(std::tan(std::numbers::pi / 16), 2));
  plot.abline_h(0, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 0.8)
      .y_limits(-13, 24)
      .title("Relative error by direction")
      .x_label("angle from the larger axis (degrees)")
      .y_label("error (%)")
      .size(860, 520)
      .save_svg("build/alpha_max_beta_min_ratio.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| pair | alpha | beta | lowest ratio | highest ratio | worst error |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| minimax | 0.96043 | 0.39782 | 0.96043 | 1.03957 | 3.96% |
| least squares | 0.94754 | 0.39249 | 0.94754 | 1.02561 | 5.25% |
| never under | 1.00000 | 0.41421 | 1.00000 | 1.08239 | 8.24% |
| (1, 1/2) | 1.00000 | 0.50000 | 1.00000 | 1.11803 | 11.80% |
| (1, 1/4) | 1.00000 | 0.25000 | 0.88388 | 1.03078 | 11.61% |
| (15/16, 15/32) | 0.93750 | 0.46875 | 0.93750 | 1.04816 | 6.25% |

tan^2(pi/16) = 0.039566.
\image(figures/alpha_max_beta_min_ratio.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Relative error of each coefficient pair against the angle of the vector, folded into \(0^\circ\) to \(45^\circ\). Each curve is one arch of a cosine. The minimax arch (thick blue) is centred at \(22.5^\circ\) and swings equally above and below zero.)
\alttext(Six arched curves of percentage error against angle from 0 to 45 degrees. The thick blue minimax curve starts at minus 3.96 percent, rises to plus 3.96 at 22.5 degrees and falls back to minus 3.96 at 45 degrees. The amber never-underestimate curve has the same shape shifted up, from 0 to 8.24 percent. The red (1, 1/2) curve rises from 0 to about 11.8 percent near 26.6 degrees and ends near 6 percent. The purple (1, 1/4) curve falls from 3 percent near 14 degrees to about minus 11.6 percent at 45 degrees. The green least-squares curve lies just below the blue one, and the grey 15/16 curve runs from minus 6 percent to about plus 4.8.)

The table confirms the algebra. The minimax pair's lowest ratio is exactly
\(\alpha\) (the axis value) and its highest is \(1+\tan^2(\pi/16)\). The
popular \((1,\tfrac12)\) never underestimates, but it overestimates by
\(\sqrt{1.25}-1=11.8\%\) at \(\tan\theta=\tfrac12\), almost three times the
minimax error. The shift-only pair \((\tfrac{15}{16},\tfrac{15}{32})\) is a
good compromise at 6.25%. The least-squares pair has a larger worst error,
5.25%, because it accepts a large error near the axes to fit the middle of
the octant better.

>> More lines: the polygon estimate

One linear form is a single arch over the octant. With \(k\) forms, split
the octant into \(k\) sectors of width \(\pi/4k\). Give sector \(j\) its
own centred arch, \(\varphi_j=(2j+1)\pi/8k\) and
\(A=2/(1+\cos(\pi/8k))\). Each arch then reaches \(1-\varepsilon_k\) at the
edges of its sector. Outside its sector a form is *smaller* than the
sector's own form, because \(A\cos(\theta-\varphi_j)\) falls as \(\theta\)
moves away from \(\varphi_j\). So the estimate is simply the largest of the
\(k\) forms, and no angle test is needed:

$$
\hat r=\max_{j}\big(\alpha_j\,\text{hi}+\beta_j\,\text{lo}\big),\qquad
\varepsilon_k=\tan^2\frac{\pi}{16k}\approx\frac{\pi^2}{256\,k^2}.
$$
\alttext(r hat is the maximum over j of alpha j hi plus beta j lo, and epsilon k equals tan squared of pi over 16 k, approximately pi squared over 256 k squared.)

Geometrically, the curve \(\hat r=1\) is a regular polygon with \(k\) sides
per octant, \(8k\) in all, squeezed between the circles of radius
\(1/(1+\varepsilon_k)\) and \(1/(1-\varepsilon_k)\). Each extra line
costs two multiplications, an addition and a comparison. Doubling \(k\)
cuts the error by four.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/alpha_max_beta_min_polygon.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::print("| lines k | worst error, measured | tan^2(pi/16k) | pi^2/(256k^2) |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> ks, measured, theory;
  for (int k = 1; k <= 8; ++k) {
    const auto lines = alg::alpha_max_beta_min_polygon(k);
    double worst = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i <= 100000; ++i) {
      const double t = 2 * std::numbers::pi * i / 100000;
      worst = std::max(worst, std::fabs(alg::alpha_max_beta_min(std::cos(t), std::sin(t), lines) - 1));
    }
    const double eps = std::pow(std::tan(std::numbers::pi / (16 * k)), 2);
    std::print("| {} | {:.3e} | {:.3e} | {:.3e} |\n", k, worst, eps,
               std::numbers::pi * std::numbers::pi / (256.0 * k * k));
    ks.push_back(std::log10(k));
    measured.push_back(std::log10(worst));
    theory.push_back(std::log10(eps));
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(ks, theory, "tan^2(pi/16k)", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
      .points(ks, measured, "measured", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 5.0)
      .title("Worst error of the k-line estimate")
      .x_label("log10 k")
      .y_label("log10 worst relative error")
      .size(860, 480)
      .save_svg("build/alpha_max_beta_min_polygon.svg");
}
```
