\image(figures/alpha_max_beta_min_polygon.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Worst relative error of the \(k\)-line polygon estimate over 100 001 directions (red points) against \(\tan^2(\pi/16k)\) (blue line), on log-log axes. The slope is \(-2\).)
\alttext(Log-log plot: red points for k from 1 to 8 lie on a straight blue line of slope minus 2, falling from log10 of 0.0396, about minus 1.4, at k equal 1 to about minus 3.2 at k equal 8.)

The measured worst errors agree with \(\tan^2(\pi/16k)\) to all four
printed digits for every \(k\), and \(\pi^2/256k^2\) is already within 0.5%
of it from \(k=2\). Two lines bring the error below 1%, and four lines
below 0.25%.

> Edge strengths of the digits

Now the real data. For each pair the next block computes the relative error
on all 100 139 gradient vectors, and the edge decisions
\(\hat r>\tau\) against \(r>\tau\) for three thresholds.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/alpha_max_beta_min_digits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::vector<double> gx, gy, len, angle;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < digits.nrows(); ++r) {
    std::vector<double> img(64);
    for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i) img[i] = digits.double_at(std::format("pixel_{}", i), r);
    const auto g = dm::cv::sobel_gradient(img, 8, 8);
    for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i)
      if (g.gx[i] != 0 || g.gy[i] != 0) {
        gx.push_back(g.gx[i]);
        gy.push_back(g.gy[i]);
        len.push_back(std::hypot(g.gx[i], g.gy[i]));
        const double a = std::fabs(g.gx[i]), b = std::fabs(g.gy[i]);
        angle.push_back(std::atan2(std::min(a, b), std::max(a, b)) * 180 / std::numbers::pi);
      }
  }

  const std::vector<std::pair<std::string, alg::AlphaBetaCoefficients>> pairs = {
      {"minimax", alg::alpha_max_beta_min_minimax()},
      {"least squares", alg::alpha_max_beta_min_least_squares()},
      {"never under", alg::alpha_max_beta_min_no_underestimate()},
      {"(1, 1/2)", {1.0, 0.5}}};
  std::print("| pair | mean error | RMS error | worst error | edge decisions changed, tau = 2 / 4 / 8 |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for (const auto& [name, c] : pairs) {
    double sum = 0, sq = 0, worst = 0;
    long changed[3] = {0, 0, 0};
    const double taus[3] = {2, 4, 8};
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < gx.size(); ++i) {
      const double est = alg::alpha_max_beta_min(gx[i], gy[i], c);
      const double e = est / len[i] - 1;
      sum += e; sq += e * e; worst = std::max(worst, std::fabs(e));
      for (int t = 0; t < 3; ++t) changed[t] += (est > taus[t]) != (len[i] > taus[t]);
    }
    const double n = static_cast<double>(gx.size());
    std::print("| {} | {:+.2f}% | {:.2f}% | {:.4f}% | {:.1f}% / {:.1f}% / {:.1f}% |\n", name, 100 * sum / n,
               100 * std::sqrt(sq / n), 100 * worst, 100 * changed[0] / n, 100 * changed[1] / n,
               100 * changed[2] / n);
  }
  long above[3] = {0, 0, 0};
  for (double l : len) for (int t = 0; t < 3; ++t) above[t] += l > (t == 0 ? 2 : t == 1 ? 4 : 8);
  std::print("\nTrue edges: {} / {} / {} vectors for tau = 2 / 4 / 8; exactly 8: {}.\n", above[0], above[1],
             above[2], std::ranges::count(len, 8.0));

  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::hist(angle, 9)
                 .title("Gradient directions")
                 .x_label("angle from the larger axis (degrees)")
                 .y_label("vectors"));
  std::vector<double> sx, sy;
  const auto mm = alg::alpha_max_beta_min_minimax();
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < gx.size(); i += 7) {
    sx.push_back(len[i]);
    sy.push_back(alg::alpha_max_beta_min(gx[i], gy[i], mm));
  }
  const double eps = std::pow(std::tan(std::numbers::pi / 16), 2);
  layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                 .points(sx, sy, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
                 .abline(0, 1 + eps, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.0)
                 .abline(0, 1 - eps, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.0)
                 .title("Minimax estimate against truth")
                 .x_label("exact edge strength")
                 .y_label("estimate"));
  layout.size(1200, 480).save_svg("build/alpha_max_beta_min_digits.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| pair | mean error | RMS error | worst error | edge decisions changed, tau = 2 / 4 / 8 |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| minimax | +0.90% | 2.88% | 3.9566% | 0.5% / 1.2% / 2.0% |
| least squares | -0.45% | 2.74% | 5.2456% | 0.4% / 1.4% / 1.6% |
| never under | +5.06% | 5.81% | 8.2392% | 1.2% / 2.5% / 3.9% |
| (1, 1/2) | +8.16% | 8.80% | 11.8034% | 1.6% / 3.4% / 5.5% |

True edges: 78086 / 53592 / 2596 vectors for tau = 2 / 4 / 8; exactly 8: 200.
\image(figures/alpha_max_beta_min_digits.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: directions of the digit gradients folded into \(0^\circ\) to \(45^\circ\). Right: the minimax estimate against the exact edge strength for every seventh vector, with the lines \(\hat r=(1\pm\varepsilon)r\) in red. Every point lies between them.)
\alttext(Two panels. Left, a histogram of gradient angle in nine bins from 0 to 45 degrees; the counts are fairly even, roughly 7 600 to 14 600 per bin, highest in the last bin, which contains the many exactly diagonal vectors. Right, a scatter of estimate against exact strength from 0 to about 9, with points forming a thin wedge between two red lines through the origin of slopes 1.04 and 0.96.)

On the real vectors the worst error of each pair is its theoretical value,
to the four decimals printed. The digits contain vectors on the axes and
on the diagonal, the exact angles where the arch is lowest, so the bound
is reached. The *mean* error depends on how directions are distributed.
For directions uniform in angle, the minimax arch averages
\(A\,\sin(\pi/8)/(\pi/8)=1.0130\), a bias of \(+1.3\%\). The digits give
\(+0.90\%\), a little less, because the tall final bin of the histogram
holds the diagonal vectors, where the estimate is 3.96% *low*. The
least-squares pair has the smallest mean and RMS error here (\(-0.45\%\)
and 2.74%), at the price of a larger worst case.

Edge decisions are where errors matter in practice. With a threshold of
\(\tau=2\), 0.5% of vectors switch side under the minimax estimate. At
\(\tau=8\) it is 2.0%, which is large next to the 2 596 vectors that are
true edges at that level. The intensities are integers and Sobel
derivatives are multiples of 1/8, so many strengths sit at or near round
values, 200 of them exactly at 8, and a 4% error moves whole clusters across
the threshold. A bias-free choice of coefficients does not remove this. If
a threshold must match the exact one, compare \(\hat r\) against
\(\tau(1\pm\varepsilon)\) and fall back to the exact magnitude only inside
that band.

>> Is it still faster?

The trick was invented for hardware without a fast square root. A modern
desktop CPU has one, so it is worth measuring. The block times 100 passes
over all the gradient vectors with each method. Times vary from run to run
and between machines, so the numbers are indicative only.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::vector<double> gx, gy;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < digits.nrows(); ++r) {
    std::vector<double> img(64);
    for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i) img[i] = digits.double_at(std::format("pixel_{}", i), r);
    const auto g = dm::cv::sobel_gradient(img, 8, 8);
    for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i)
      if (g.gx[i] != 0 || g.gy[i] != 0) { gx.push_back(g.gx[i]); gy.push_back(g.gy[i]); }
  }
  const auto c = alg::alpha_max_beta_min_minimax();
  const auto poly = alg::alpha_max_beta_min_polygon(2);
  auto time = [&](auto&& f) {
    volatile double sink = 0;
    const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    for (int rep = 0; rep < 100; ++rep) {
      double s = 0;
      for (std::size_t i = 0; i < gx.size(); ++i) s += f(gx[i], gy[i]);
      sink = sink + s;
    }
    const std::chrono::duration<double, std::nano> dt = std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0;
    return dt.count() / (100.0 * gx.size());
  };
  std::print("| method | ns per vector |\n| :--- | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| std::hypot | {:.2f} |\n", time([](double x, double y) { return std::hypot(x, y); }));
  std::print("| std::sqrt(x*x + y*y) | {:.2f} |\n", time([](double x, double y) { return std::sqrt(x * x + y * y); }));
  std::print("| alpha max beta min | {:.2f} |\n", time([&](double x, double y) { return alg::alpha_max_beta_min(x, y, c); }));
  std::print("| 2-line polygon | {:.2f} |\n", time([&](double x, double y) { return alg::alpha_max_beta_min(x, y, poly); }));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| method | ns per vector |
| :--- | ---: |
| std::hypot | 8.02 |
| std::sqrt(x*x + y*y) | 1.61 |
| alpha max beta min | 2.52 |
| 2-line polygon | 2.60 |
// result_end

On the machine that built this report, the estimate was *slower* than
`std::sqrt(x*x + y*y)`: about 2 ns against 1.5 ns per vector. Modern CPUs
compute a double-precision square root in a few cycles and pipeline it
well, while the estimate needs absolute values, a comparison and a
selection. The estimate was still about three times faster than
`std::hypot`. That is the fairer comparison, because the estimate shares
`hypot`'s robustness: it never squares its inputs, so it cannot overflow
or underflow where \(x^2+y^2\) would. Alpha max plus beta min still pays
off where a square root is expensive, as on fixed-point DSPs, FPGAs and
small microcontrollers. It also helps where only comparisons between
magnitudes are needed. On a desktop CPU, `std::sqrt` wins.

> Summary

- In polar form the estimate's ratio to the truth is
  \(A\cos(\theta-\varphi)\), one arch of a cosine over an octant. Any pair's
  exact error range follows from the arch's ends and peak.
- Centring the arch gives the minimax pair
  \(\alpha=2\cos(\pi/8)/(1+\cos(\pi/8))\),
  \(\beta=2\sin(\pi/8)/(1+\cos(\pi/8))\), with worst error
  \(\tan^2(\pi/16)=3.96\%\). Moving it up to touch 1 gives the
  never-underestimating pair \((1,\tan(\pi/8))\) at 8.24%.
- The maximum of \(k\) centred arches has error \(\tan^2(\pi/16k)\), falling
  as \(1/k^2\), and needs no angle test.
- On 100 139 digit gradients the worst observed error equals the theory.
  Because directions fill the octant, the minimax pair overestimates by
  0.90% on average.

```{cpp, eval="no"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

double r1 = alg::alpha_max_beta_min(3.0, 4.0);                     // minimax pair, ~4% error
auto   c  = alg::alpha_max_beta_min_no_underestimate();           // (1, tan(pi/8))
auto   e  = alg::alpha_max_beta_min_error(c);                     // e.min_ratio, e.max_ratio
double r2 = alg::alpha_max_beta_min(3.0, 4.0, alg::alpha_max_beta_min_polygon(4));  // 0.24% error
```
